  "Question": "Two numbers whose sum is \\(6\\) and the absolute value of whose difference is \\(8\\) are roots of the equation:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(x^2-6x+7=0\\)",
    "B": "\\(x^2-6x-7=0\\)",
    "C": "\\(x^2+6x-8=0\\)",
    "D": "\\(x^2-6x+8=0\\)",
    "E": "\\(x^2+6x-7=0\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "B",
  "Solution": "Call the larger number \\(u\\) and the smaller \\(v\\). Ordering them turns the absolute difference into \\(u-v=8\\), so\n\\[\nu+v=6,\\qquad u-v=8.\n\\]\nAdding these equations eliminates \\(v\\): \\(2u=14\\), giving \\(u=7\\). Then \\(v=6-7=-1\\). Thus the pair is determined up to order.\n\nTo build an equation with these roots, use one factor that vanishes at each number:\n\\[\n(x-7)(x+1)=0.\n\\]\nA product is zero exactly when at least one factor is zero, so this equation has precisely the required roots. Expanding gives\n\\[\n(x-7)(x+1)=x^2-6x-7.\n\\]\nThe required equation is \\(x^2-6x-7=0\\), choice B.",
